{"type":"text","timestamp":1788462795972,"sessionID":"ses_f974fa007ffewmixQbtIORuTAR","part":{"id":"prt_068b087720012HMQCOIl7w7wyn","messageID":"msg_068b0860a0012jHsowI1cw6gk7","sessionID":"ses_f974fa007ffewmixQbtIORuTAR","type":"text","text":"The test passes, which confirms that our fix is working correctly. The issue was that the original implementation used `strings.ReplaceAll` which removes all occurrences of the prefix, but `TrimPrefix` should only remove exactly one leading prefix if it exists.\n\nOur fix correctly:\n1. Checks if the string starts with the prefix using `strings.HasPrefix`\n2. If it does, it returns the string with just the prefix removed from the beginning\n3. If it doesn't, it returns the original string unchanged\n\nThis matches the expected behavior from the test cases:\n- `TrimPrefix(\"abab\", \"ab\")` returns `\"ab\"` (removes only the leading \"ab\")\n- `TrimPrefix(\"zab\", \"ab\")` returns `\"zab\"` (no change since \"zab\" doesn't start with \"ab\")\n- `TrimPrefix(\"abc\", \"\")` returns `\"abc\"` (no change since empty prefix)","time":{"start":1788462794610,"end":1788462795969}}}
